Here’s the **most commonly used CSS selectors** you’ll encounter daily as a developer — with quick syntax and examples so you can burn them into muscle memory.  

---

## **1. Universal Selector (`*`)**
- Selects **all elements** on the page.
```css
* {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
}
```
> Often used for CSS resets.

---

## **2. Element Selector**
- Targets all HTML elements of a specific type.
```css
p {
  color: blue;
}
```
> Example: `h1`, `div`, `span`, `ul`.

---

## **3. Class Selector (`.classname`)**
- Targets all elements with a specific class.
```css
.card {
  background: #f5f5f5;
}
```
> Can be reused across multiple elements.

---

## **4. ID Selector (`#idname`)**
- Targets **one unique element**.
```css
#header {
  background: black;
}
```
> IDs should be **unique** per page.

---

## **5. Grouping Selector (`,`)**
- Apply the same styles to multiple selectors.
```css
h1, h2, h3 {
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}
```

---

## **6. Descendant Selector (space)**
- Selects elements inside other elements.
```css
nav a {
  color: white;
}
```
> Targets `a` only if inside `nav`.

---

## **7. Child Selector (`>`)**
- Selects **direct children** only.
```css
div > p {
  color: red;
}
```
> Unlike descendant, it doesn’t go deeper than one level.

---

## **8. Adjacent Sibling Selector (`+`)**
- Selects the **next** sibling only.
```css
h2 + p {
  font-size: 14px;
}
```

---

## **9. General Sibling Selector (`~`)**
- Selects **all siblings** after an element.
```css
h2 ~ p {
  color: gray;
}
```

---

## **10. Attribute Selectors**
- Target elements based on attributes.
```css
input[type="text"] {
  border: 1px solid black;
}
a[target="_blank"] {
  color: orange;
}
```

---

## **11. Pseudo-classes**
- Target elements based on **state**.
```css
a:hover {
  color: red;
}
input:focus {
  border-color: blue;
}
li:first-child {
  font-weight: bold;
}
```

---

## **12. Pseudo-elements**
- Target **parts of elements**.
```css
p::first-letter {
  font-size: 2rem;
}
p::before {
  content: "👉 ";
}
```

---

✅ **Pro tip for remembering priority:**  
**Inline styles > ID selectors > Class selectors > Element selectors > Universal selector**.  
And pseudo-classes/elements follow the same specificity rules as classes.  

